</span><span><span class="hljs-section">## Appendix — Gradient–Closure Equivalence</span></span><span>

In </span><span><span class="hljs-strong">**RAT**</span></span><span>, equilibrium occurs when \( \nabla R(x^<span class="hljs-emphasis">*) = 0 \).  
In </span></span><span><span class="hljs-strong">**ATT**</span></span><span>, equilibrium occurs when \( T(X^*) = X^* \) and \( T^2 = T \).

Linearizing ATT:
\[
\delta X</span><span><span class="hljs-emphasis">_{n+1} = J_</span></span><span>T(X) \, \delta X<span class="hljs-emphasis">_n
\]
Idempotence implies \( J_</span>T^2 = J<span class="hljs-emphasis">_T \): a projection operator.

In RAT, the Hessian at the fixed point acts similarly, projecting dynamics onto the stable subspace.  
Thus:

\[
T^2 = T \;\Longleftrightarrow\; \nabla R = 0
\]

Both describe </span></span><span><span class="hljs-strong">**projection onto self-consistent equilibrium**</span></span><span>.

