0.8276 8.50176 11.4982 9.17239 11.4984 9.99963C11.4984 10.827 10.8277 11.4985 10.0003 11.4987C9.17283 11.4987 8.50131 10.8271 8.50131 9.99963C8.50149 9.17228 9.17294 8.50159 10.0003 8.50159Z"></path></svg></button></div></div></div></div></article><article class="text-token-text-primary w-full focus:outline-none [--shadow-height:45px] has-data-writing-block:pointer-events-none has-data-writing-block:-mt-(--shadow-height) has-data-writing-block:pt-(--shadow-height) [&amp;:has([data-writing-block])&gt;*]:pointer-events-auto scroll-mt-(--header-height)" tabindex="-1" dir="auto" data-turn-id="40c10f87-5c21-4c6a-babe-ad54f7725cd6" data-testid="conversation-turn-11" data-scroll-anchor="false" data-turn="user"><h5 class="sr-only">You said:</h5><div class="text-base my-auto mx-auto pt-12 [--thread-content-margin:--spacing(4)] thread-sm:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(6)] thread-lg:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(16)] px-(--thread-content-margin)"><div class="[--thread-content-max-width:40rem] thread-lg:[--thread-content-max-width:48rem] mx-auto max-w-(--thread-content-max-width) flex-1 group/turn-messages focus-visible:outline-hidden relative flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col" tabindex="-1"><div class="flex max-w-full flex-col grow"><div data-message-author-role="user" data-message-id="40c10f87-5c21-4c6a-babe-ad54f7725cd6" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-1"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden items-end rtl:items-start"><div class="user-message-bubble-color relative rounded-[18px] px-4 py-1.5 data-[multiline]:py-3 max-w-[var(--user-chat-width,70%)]" data-multiline=""><div class="whitespace-pre-wrap">An Introduction to Autogeneration: How Systems Know Themselves

How does anything maintain its identity? Consider a person learning a new skill, a story unfolding one sentence at a time, or even a galaxy forming over billions of years. Each one is a dynamic process, constantly changing and interacting with its environment, yet it holds onto a coherent form. How does a system know how to continue being itself, moment after moment?

This question leads us to a profound principle called autogeneration: the idea that systems generate their own futures based on the entire path of their past. While the principle is simple, it is supported by a vast mathematical machinery—from information geometry to higher-dimensional category theory—that aims to describe a universal law of coherence. Unlike simple feedback loops that react only to the most recent event (a Markovian process), autogenerative systems are deeply non-Markovian; their future is shaped by the memory of their complete history.

The central thesis of this concept is a simple yet powerful statement:

To know is to remain self-consistent under transformation.

Let's break that down. "Transformation" can mean the simple passage of time, the arrival of new information, or interaction with the outside world. "Self-consistent" means maintaining a core identity, a stable structure, or a coherent pattern without falling apart. In this view, knowing isn't about storing static facts; it's the active, dynamic process of preserving who or what you are in the face of constant change.

To make this abstract idea more tangible, let's explore it through a powerful analogy from cognitive science.

2. The Central Pattern Generator: An Analogy for Self-Consistency

Consider the act of walking. You don't consciously decide to lift your foot, swing your leg, and place it down with every step. The rhythm is managed by Central Pattern Generators (CPGs)—oscillatory neural circuits in your spinal cord that produce coherent, rhythmic outputs.

A CPG is in a state of constant transformation—neurons are firing in a ceaseless, cyclical pattern. Yet, despite this activity, it maintains a stable identity: the unwavering rhythm of walking.

* The CPG "knows" how to walk by continuously maintaining this self-consistent pattern of neural firing. Its existence is the stable rhythm it generates.
* If you stumble or the rhythm is disrupted, the CPG has lost its self-consistency. For a moment, it is no longer generating a "walking" pattern; its identity has been challenged by the transformation of interacting with the world.

Like a CPG, any system that "knows" something—whether it's a person who knows how to ride a bike or a planet that knows its orbit—has found a stable, self-reinforcing pattern of existence that it can successfully maintain as it moves through time. This stability is not static; it is a dynamic, self-reinforcing dance.

But how does a system find and follow these stable patterns? It does so by carving a path through a landscape of possibilities.

3. The Path of Coherence: How Autogeneration Works

Picture any system's history as a path carved into a vast "landscape of possibilities." This groove is its memory. Every step, every moment, deepens this path, making it easier for the future to follow the same memory-driven, coherent track.

* The Path of Least Resistance: A smooth, logical, or coherent continuation—like the next logical sentence in a story or a musician playing a familiar scale—is like following the most natural, "lowest-energy" path forward. This is the system's geodesic. In physics, a geodesic is the straightest possible path an object can follow on a curved surface. For an autogenerative system, the "surface" is the landscape of possibilities, and "curvature" is surprise or incoherence. A coherent thought is simply one that follows a straight line through this informational space.
* 'Curvature' as Surprise: A surprising or incoherent event—a nonsensical sentence, a jarringly wrong note—is a sharp turn or deviation from this smooth path. This "informational curvature" creates tension and requires energy and effort to navigate. An autogenerative system must work to smooth out this curvature to maintain its self-consistency.

By naturally following these smooth, low-energy paths, autogenerative systems are incredibly efficient. They preserve their coherence over time by continuing what they have been, making the expected next step the most likely one. This process of following a coherent path eventually leads to a state of deep stability we can call understanding.

4. Arriving at Understanding: The Stable Fixed Point

What does it mean to finally "understand" something? In the autogenerative view, understanding is the moment a system reaches a stable equilibrium—a state that reinforces itself. Think of a chaotic, flooding river that eventually settles into a steady, powerful, and predictable flow. That steady flow is a state of equilibrium.

This brings us to the concept of a fixed point, or idempotence.

An operation is idempotent if applying it more than once has no further effect. For example, pressing the "bold" button on text that is already bold doesn't change it. The state is fixed. This is precisely what happens when a system achieves understanding: new, similar information is easily assimilated without changing the core concept because the system has found such a stable pattern that further transformations of the same kind no longer disrupt its identity.

This leads to a powerful conclusion about the nature of reality itself.

Understanding is the unique fixed point of reality.

This means that the ultimate state toward which self-consistent systems evolve is one of stable, coherent comprehension. It is the destination at the end of the path of coherence.

5. Autogeneration in Action: From Brains to Galaxies

This principle isn't just an abstract idea; it appears to be a fundamental organizing rule across vastly different domains. Here are a few examples:

1. Learning and Cognition: When a person learns a new language, their speech is initially halting and full of errors (high informational curvature). With practice, they carve a coherent path. Eventually, their knowledge becomes so self-consistent that they can generate new, grammatically correct sentences effortlessly. The conscious effort of applying rules becomes automatic. This cognitive process is called aspect relegation, and it is the direct manifestation of understanding reaching an idempotent fixed point.
2. Computation and AI: A generative AI writing a story must follow a coherent path. Each new paragraph must be self-consistent with the established characters, plot, and tone. If the AI generates a sentence that contradicts the story's history, it represents a failure of its non-Markovian memory—it has failed to maintain self-consistency. The most compelling stories follow a smooth, low-energy geodesic through the space of narrative possibilities.
3. Physics and Nature: A spiral galaxy is a breathtaking example of autogeneration. It is a persistent, coherent structure that maintains its shape for billions of years, even as the stars, gas, and dust that comprise it are in constant motion and are continually being replaced. The galaxy remains self-consistent under the ceaseless transformation of cosmic evolution, a stable pattern on a cosmological scale.

These examples, from the neural to the cosmological, all point toward the same underlying principle.

6. Conclusion: A Unifying View of the World

We began with a simple question: how do things stay themselves? The autogenerative principle offers a profound answer: the ability to exist, to learn, and to understand is fundamentally linked to the process of maintaining a coherent identity through constant change. To know is to be able to continue being yourself.

This idea is powerful because it suggests a glimpse into a fundamental law of nature. Just as the principle of least action governs physical motion, the principle of autogeneration may govern cognitive and informational motion—a universal drive to maintain coherence against the tide of change. It is a kind of cognitive inertia, a conservation law for information itself.

As you look at the world, from the way you learn a skill to the formation of a galaxy, you can now see it through this new lens—as a universe of systems engaged in the elegant, universal process of knowing themselves.</div></div></div></div></div><div class="z-0 flex justify-end"><div class="touch:-me-2 touch:-ms-3.5 -ms-2.5 -me-1 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-y-4 p-1 select-none focus-within:transition-none hover:transition-none touch:pointer-events-auto touch:opacity-100 duration-300 group-hover/turn-messages:delay-300 pointer-events-none opacity-0 motion-safe:transition-opacity group-hover/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-hover/turn-messages:opacity-100 group-focus-within/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-focus-within/turn-messages:opacity-100 has-data-[state=open]:pointer-events-auto has-data-[state=open]:opacity-100" style="mask-position: 0% 0%;"><button class="text-token-text-secondary hover:bg-token-bg-secondary rounded-lg" aria-label="Copy" aria-pressed="false" data-testid="copy-turn-action-button" data-state="closed"><span class="flex items-center justify-center touch:w-10 h-8 w-8"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="icon"><path d="M12.668 10.667C12.668 9.95614 12.668 9.46258 12.6367 9.0791C12.6137 8.79732 12.5758 8.60761 12.5244 8.46387L12.4688 8.33399C12.3148 8.03193 12.0803 7.77885 11.793 7.60254L11.666 7.53125C11.508 7.45087 11.2963 7.39395 10.9209 7.36328C10.5374 7.33197 10.0439 7.33203 9.33301 7.33203H6.5C5.78896 7.33203 5.29563 7.33195 4.91211 7.36328C4.63016 7.38632 4.44065 7.42413 4.29688 7.47559L4.16699 7.53125C3.86488 7.68518 3.61186 7.9196 3.43555 8.20703L3.36524 8.33399C3.28478 8.49198 3.22795 8.70352 3.19727 9.0791C3.16595 9.46259 3.16504 9.95611 3.16504 10.667V13.5C3.16504 14.211 3.16593 14.7044 3.19727 15.0879C3.22797 15.4636 3.28473 15.675 3.36524 15.833L3.43555 15.959C3.61186 16.2466 3.86474 16.4807 4.16699 16.6348L4.29688 16.6914C4.44063 16.7428 4.63