Where the term </span><span><span class="hljs-code">`λ‖a‖₁`</span></span><span> forces </span><span><span class="hljs-strong">**sparsity**</span></span><span> — selecting only a tiny set of “policy atoms.”

This creates a </span><span><span class="hljs-strong">**winner-takes-few**</span></span><span> effect:

</span><span><span class="hljs-quote">&gt; If a thought isn’t valuable enough to justify its cost, it doesn’t just get weak — it gets deleted.</span></span><span>

Cognition is not:
</span><span><span class="hljs-bullet">-</span></span><span> “consider everything then choose”

Cognition is:
</span><span><span class="hljs-bullet">-</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-strong">**eliminate almost everything, instantly**</span></span><span>

---

</span><span><span class="hljs-section">## 6. A Tale of Two Skulls — Why Structure Matters</span></span><span>

Two AI systems were trained to generate a skull:

| System | Result | Internal Representation | Meaning |
|---|---|---|---|
| </span><span><span class="hljs-strong">**Gradient descent (SGD)**</span></span><span> | ✔ produced skull | chaotic, entangled, brittle | memorized correlations |
| </span><span><span class="hljs-strong">**Evolutionary search (Picbreeder)**</span></span><span> | ✔ produced skull | sparse, modular, interpretable | discovered causal structure |

The second system learned true generative factors like:
</span><span><span class="hljs-bullet">-</span></span><span> Bilateral symmetry
</span><span><span class="hljs-bullet">-</span></span><span> Jaw shape
</span><span><span class="hljs-bullet">-</span></span><span> Modular parts

A single parameter change could mean:
</span><span><span class="hljs-quote">&gt; “open the mouth wider”  </span></span><span>
instead of  
</span><span><span class="hljs-quote">&gt; “produce nonsense”</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-section">### Lesson:</span></span><span>
Sparse, structured cognition discovers </span><span><span class="hljs-strong">**causal truth**</span></span><span>.  
Dense, entangled cognition memorizes </span><span><span class="hljs-strong">**statistical surface texture**</span></span><span>.

Human thought works like the first </span><span><span class="hljs-emphasis">*only when sick*</span></span><span> (rumination, confusion, noise).  
It works like the second </span><span><span class="hljs-emphasis">*when healthy*</span></span><span> (clarity, insight, abstraction).

---

</span><span><span class="hljs-section">## 7. Conclusion — The Efficient, Curious Mind</span></span><span>

Thinking is not mystical. It is:

</span><span><span class="hljs-bullet">-</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-strong">**sparse**</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-bullet">-</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-strong">**energy-efficient**</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-bullet">-</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-strong">**physics-constrained**</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-bullet">-</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-strong">**causally structured**</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-bullet">-</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-strong">**balancing action &amp; learning**</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-bullet">-</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-strong">**selecting one path out of infinite possibilities**</span></span><span>

You do not think by enumerating options.  
You think by </span><span><span class="hljs-strong">**pruning almost all options and letting one survive**</span></span><span>.

Understanding, then, is:

</span><span><span class="hljs-quote">&gt; <span class="hljs-strong">**The emergence of a low-entropy, causally structured, sparsely selected path through the landscape of possibility.**</span></span></span><span>

