018 9.17239 3.6708 8.50176 4.49805 8.50159Z"></path><path d="M10.0003 8.50159C10.8276 8.50176 11.4982 9.17239 11.4984 9.99963C11.4984 10.827 10.8277 11.4985 10.0003 11.4987C9.17283 11.4987 8.50131 10.8271 8.50131 9.99963C8.50149 9.17228 9.17294 8.50159 10.0003 8.50159Z"></path></svg></button></div></div></div></div></article><article class="text-token-text-primary w-full focus:outline-none [--shadow-height:45px] has-data-writing-block:pointer-events-none has-data-writing-block:-mt-(--shadow-height) has-data-writing-block:pt-(--shadow-height) [&amp;:has([data-writing-block])&gt;*]:pointer-events-auto scroll-mt-(--header-height)" tabindex="-1" dir="auto" data-turn-id="85cdfa33-1f78-4388-a696-d47ffcf34da2" data-testid="conversation-turn-33" data-scroll-anchor="false" data-turn="user"><h5 class="sr-only">You said:</h5><div class="text-base my-auto mx-auto pt-12 [--thread-content-margin:--spacing(4)] thread-sm:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(6)] thread-lg:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(16)] px-(--thread-content-margin)"><div class="[--thread-content-max-width:40rem] thread-lg:[--thread-content-max-width:48rem] mx-auto max-w-(--thread-content-max-width) flex-1 group/turn-messages focus-visible:outline-hidden relative flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col" tabindex="-1"><div class="flex max-w-full flex-col grow"><div data-message-author-role="user" data-message-id="85cdfa33-1f78-4388-a696-d47ffcf34da2" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-1"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden items-end rtl:items-start"><div class="user-message-bubble-color relative rounded-[18px] px-4 py-1.5 data-[multiline]:py-3 max-w-[var(--user-chat-width,70%)]" data-multiline=""><div class="whitespace-pre-wrap">Why Your Brain, Social Media, and AI All Obey the Same Laws of Physics

We live in a time of accelerating confusion. We grapple with the challenge of aligning artificial intelligence, navigate the disorienting dynamics of social media, and still ponder the fundamental mysteries of our own consciousness. These problems feel vast, complex, and, above all, separate.

But what if they aren't? What if a single, underlying framework—a kind of physics for meaning itself—could connect them all?

A series of radical theoretical papers from a project called Flyxion proposes exactly this: a unified model where thought, AI, and social systems are all governed by the physical laws of information, entropy, and energy. This framework treats cognition not as abstract computation, but as a physical process unfolding in a universe of meaning.

This post will distill the five most surprising and impactful takeaways from this new "physics of thought," revealing the hidden laws that govern our digital and mental worlds.

1. Thinking Isn't Magic. It's a Physical Process with a Strict Budget.

The theory begins with a foundational claim: reasoning, agency, and even a single thought are not abstract computations but physical processes unfolding in a “semantic plenum”—a field of meaning. This isn't just an academic distinction; it's a fundamental re-evaluation of what a "thought" even is. It moves cognition from the realm of software to the realm of physics, with all the hard limits that implies.

Specifically, thought operates under two strict budgets:

* Sparsity: Every cognitive process has a "metabolic cost." Activating a concept or firing a neuron requires energy. Therefore, any effective thought process must be economical, using the fewest possible components to achieve a goal. This is formalized using a LASSO-style objective, which enforces parsimony. You can only think about a few things at once because you literally can't afford to think about everything at once.
* Entropy: Cognition is also constrained by an "entropy budget," a limit on how much disorder or incoherence a thought process can tolerate before it dissolves into randomness. This budget ensures that ideas maintain their structure and don't drift into meaninglessness.

This reframes the concept of "bounded rationality." It isn't a psychological flaw or a cognitive shortcut; it is a fundamental law of "cognitive economy." Our minds aren't lazy; they are forced to be efficient and sparse by the same principles that govern energy and information in the universe.

But if our own minds are governed by this strict cognitive economy, what happens when we build systems that ignore these laws entirely? This brings us to the chaotic thermodynamics of social media.

2. Your Social Feed Obeys the Laws of Extraction (and It's Making You Work Harder for Less).

The frustrating experience of posting on social media—the feeling of shouting into a void where your reach constantly diminishes—can also be described by a field theory. An "extractive platform" is defined by three components:

* Visibility Potential (Φ): Your potential to be seen.
* Agency Vector (v): Your effort—posting, creating, engaging.
* Informational Entropy (S): The chaos, noise, and unpredictability of the feed.

These components are governed by two simple but brutal equations:

1. ∇Φ · v &lt; 0: The more effort you put in (agency), the less you are seen (visibility).
2. ∇S · v &gt; 0: The more effort you put in, the more chaotic and unpredictable the system becomes.

This is the formal physics behind the feeling of "work harder, reach less." But why does it happen? The second equation reveals the mechanism: each post, like, or comment you make, while intended to promote your own content, also adds to the total volume of information the system must process and rank, increasing the overall noise and making every individual signal harder to find. On these platforms, your effort doesn't build your visibility; it increases the system's overall chaos, which in turn suppresses your reach.

This isn't a personal failure or a simple algorithmic quirk that can be tweaked. It is a foundational law of the system's design, as inevitable and impersonal as a thermodynamic process.

3. An AI Can Give the Right Answers for All the Wrong Reasons.

Imagine two AI models that produce the exact same image of a skull. From the outside, they are identical. But on the inside, they can be radically different. This distinction is at the heart of a phenomenon called "Platonic Intelligence."

When we train AI with conventional methods like Stochastic Gradient Descent (SGD), we often get internal representations that are:

* Fractured and Entangled: A complete mess internally. The model learns a "spaghetti" of connections that just happen to cancel out to produce the correct output. It doesn't understand the underlying structure of a skull; it has just memorized a complex pattern that generates one.

In contrast, open-ended, evolutionary processes (like those seen in the online experiment Picbreeder) tend to produce representations that are:

* Unified and Factored: Modular, organized, and reflective of the object's true nature. For example, a single weight might control the jaw width, or another might control the eye spacing. The model has learned the underlying symmetries of the skull.

This happens because evolutionary search implicitly rewards modularity and adaptability; a representation where "jaw width" is a single, editable knob is far more likely to survive and be built upon than a tangled mess where changing the jaw inadvertently changes the eyes.

This difference is critical. The "factored" representations are adaptable, creative, and can generalize to new situations (OOD or out-of-distribution). The "fractured" ones are brittle; change the context slightly, and the entire illusion collapses. As the source paper notes:

"two LLMs may have the same output behavior, as in you can't tell them apart from what they're doing on the outside, but their internal representation and how they see the world internally may be qualitatively different."

4. We Don't Need Nicer AIs. We Need AIs That Can't Even Conceive of Harming Us.

Current approaches to AI alignment, like Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF), are primarily "behavioral." They are like teaching a child not to steal by rewarding them for not stealing. The underlying concept of stealing, and the goal of acquiring an object, may still exist.

A far more robust approach is "Invariant Alignment." This isn't about training an AI's outputs; it's about shaping the very structure of its goals. The analogy is building a mind that doesn't have a concept of stealing in the first place.

This is described as a topological constraint. The goal is to design an AI's cognitive space—its internal world of possible thoughts and objectives—so that misaligned goals are "not representable" or "unreachable." It's like designing a landscape with no roads leading to dangerous places. No matter how much optimization pressure the AI is under, it cannot drift into a harmful state because that state simply does not exist in its world. This represents a profound paradigm shift from shaping behavior to engineering the very fabric of cognition.

This leads to a powerful new axiom for AI safety:

"A system is only safe if misaligned objectives lie outside its representational closure."

This imperative to design the very space of thought, rather than just its output, leads to the most profound insight of all: a new definition for the self.

5. Your "Self" Isn't a Thing. It's a Stable Pattern in a Sea of Chaos.

Perhaps the most profound idea in this framework is its definition of the self. Your identity is not a static object or a little person inside your head. It is an emergent phenomenon—a "long-timescale policy prior" that has managed to achieve stability in a universe that tends toward disorder.

In simpler terms, the "self" is a complex, self-reinforcing pattern of thoughts and behaviors. This pattern operates at a point of "cognitive criticality"—a delicate balance at the edge of a phase transition. Tip too far one way, and the pattern dissolves into chaos (dissociation). Tip too far the other way, and it becomes too rigid and unchanging (narcissism).

From this perspective, your identity is a literal, physical act of resistance against entropy. It is a "negentropy reservoir" that actively works to maintain its own low-entropy, highly structured pattern against the constant pressure of universal disorder. The self is not a noun; it's a verb. It is the pattern that refuses to die.

Conclusion: A New Way of Seeing

These five takeaways offer facets of a single, unified picture. We see now that cognition, both biological and artificial, is a physical process of maintaining low-entropy, sparse patterns (the "self" or a "factored" AI) within a strict energy budget. Extractive social platforms function as entropy engines that deliberately break this budget, forcing users into a chaotic, high-effort, low-reward state. True AI alignment, then, is the art of building systems whose very topology makes high-entropy, harmful states impossible to reach. It suggests that the challenges we face with our minds, our technologies, and our societies are not fundamentally different from one another.

This leads to a final, provocative question. If thought, society, and AI all obey the laws of a hidden physics, what if the biggest challenges of the 21st century are not problems of politics or psychology, but of thermodynamics?</div></div></div></div></div><div class="z-0 flex justify-end"><div class="touch:-me-2 touch:-ms-3.5 -ms-2.5 -me-1 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-y-4 p-1 select-none focus-within:transition-none hover:transition-none touch:pointer-events-auto touch:opacity-100 duration-300 group-hover/turn-messages:delay-300 pointer-events-none opacity-0 motion-safe:transition-opacity group-hover/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-hover/turn-messages:opacity-100 group-focus-within/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-focus-within/turn-messages:opacity-100 has-data-[state=open]:pointer-events-auto has-data-[state=open]:opacity-100" style="mask-position: 0% 0%;"><button class="text-token-text-secondary hover:bg-token-bg-secondary rounded-lg" aria-label="Copy" aria-pressed="false" data-testid="copy-turn-action-button" data-state="closed"><span class="flex items-center justify-center touch:w-10 h-8 w-8"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="icon"><path d="M12.668 10.667C12.668 9.95614 12.668 9.46258 12.6367 9.0791C12.6137 8.79732 12.5758 8.60761 12.5244 8.46387L12.4688 8.33399C12.3148 8.03193 12.0803 7.77885 11.793 7.60254L11.666 7.53125C11.508 7.45087 11.2963 7.39395 10.9209 7.36328C10.5374 7.33197 10.0439 7.33203 9.33301 7.33203H6.5C5.78896 7.33203 5.29563 7.33195 4.91211 7.36328C4.63016 7.38632 4.44065 7.42413 4.29688 7.47559L4.16699 7.53125C3.86488 7.68518 3.61186 7.9196 3.43555 8.20703L3.36524 8.33399C3.28478 8.49198 3.22795 8.70352 3.19727 9.0791C3.16595 9.46259 3.16504 9.95611 3.16504 10.667V13.5C3.16504 14.211 3.16593 14.7044 3.19727 15.0879C3.22797 15.4636 3.28473 15.675 3.36524 15.833L3.43555 15.959C3.61186 16.2466 3.86474 16.4807 4.16699 16.6348L4.29688 16.6914C4.44063 