 6.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-markdown"><span><span><span class="hljs-section"># Why Your Brain, Social Media, and AI All Obey the Same Laws of Physics</span></span><span>

We live in a time of accelerating confusion. We grapple with the challenge of aligning artificial intelligence, navigate the disorienting dynamics of social media, and still ponder the fundamental mysteries of our own consciousness. These problems feel vast, complex, and, above all, </span><span><span class="hljs-strong">**separate**</span></span><span>.

But what if they aren't? What if a single, underlying framework—a kind of </span><span><span class="hljs-strong">**physics for meaning itself**</span></span><span>—could connect them all?

A series of radical theoretical papers from a project called </span><span><span class="hljs-strong">**Flyxion**</span></span><span> proposes exactly this: a unified model where </span><span><span class="hljs-strong">**thought, AI, and social systems**</span></span><span> are all governed by the physical laws of </span><span><span class="hljs-strong">**information, entropy, and energy**</span></span><span>. This framework treats cognition not as abstract computation, but as a </span><span><span class="hljs-strong">**physical process unfolding in a universe of meaning**</span></span><span>.

This post will distill the five most surprising and impactful takeaways from this new </span><span><span class="hljs-emphasis">*"physics of thought,"*</span></span><span> revealing the hidden laws that govern our digital and mental worlds.

---

</span><span><span class="hljs-section">## 1. Thinking Isn't Magic. It's a Physical Process With a Strict Budget.</span></span><span>

The theory begins with a foundational claim:

</span><span><span class="hljs-quote">&gt; <span class="hljs-strong">**Reasoning, agency, and even a single thought are not abstract computations but physical processes unfolding in a "semantic plenum"—a field of meaning.**</span></span></span><span>

This moves cognition from the realm of </span><span><span class="hljs-strong">**software**</span></span><span> into the realm of </span><span><span class="hljs-strong">**physics**</span></span><span>, with all the constraints that implies.

Thought operates under two strict budgets:

</span><span><span class="hljs-bullet">-</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-strong">**Sparsity**</span></span><span> — Every cognitive operation has a metabolic cost. Neurons firing, concepts activating: all of it consumes energy. Efficient thought must therefore use the </span><span><span class="hljs-strong">**smallest possible number of components**</span></span><span> to achieve its goal. This is mathematically formalized using a </span><span><span class="hljs-emphasis">*LASSO-style objective*</span></span><span> that enforces parsimony.
</span><span><span class="hljs-bullet">-</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-strong">**Entropy**</span></span><span> — There is a limit to how much noise, disorder, or incoherence a thought process can survive before dissolving into randomness.

This reframes </span><span><span class="hljs-emphasis">*bounded rationality*</span></span><span>:

</span><span><span class="hljs-quote">&gt; It isn't a psychological flaw—it's a <span class="hljs-strong">**law of cognitive thermodynamics**</span></span></span><span>.

Our brains aren't lazy. They're </span><span><span class="hljs-strong">**forced to be sparse and efficient**</span></span><span>, because the cost of thinking is real.

---

</span><span><span class="hljs-section">## 2. Social Media Obeys Laws of Extraction (and It's Making You Work Harder for Less)</span></span><span>

The feeling of posting into the void—more effort, worse reach—can be expressed using the same physics.

Extractive platforms operate using three fields:

</span><span><span class="hljs-bullet">-</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-strong">**Visibility Potential (Φ)**</span></span><span> — your ability to be seen
</span><span><span class="hljs-bullet">-</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-strong">**Agency Vector (v)**</span></span><span> — your effort (posting, creating, engaging)
</span><span><span class="hljs-bullet">-</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-strong">**Informational Entropy (S)**</span></span><span> — chaos and competition in the feed

These obey two structural laws:

</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
<p data-start="2835" data-end="2942">∇Φ · v &lt; 0   →   More effort leads to less visibility<br data-start="2888" data-end="2891">
∇S · v &gt; 0   →   More effort increases system chaos</p>
