Glossary of Key Terms

Term	Definition
Agency Vector (v)	A vector field in the RSVP framework representing effective action flow, agentic flow, or the probability current of intentional action. In extractive platforms, its gradient is opposed to the visibility potential (∇Φ · v &lt; 0).
Behavioral Alignment	An AI alignment strategy that operates solely on a system's output distributions to make them match desired behaviors. It is considered unstable because it does not constrain the system's internal objective structure, which can drift under optimization.
Cognitive Criticality	A state in the RSVP mind model, theorized to be at the edge of a binding phase transition. At this critical point, the system exhibits scale-free policy binding, maximal susceptibility to context, optimal information transfer, and the emergence of a stable "self."
Compositional Pattern Producing Network (CPPN)	A type of neural network used as a toy domain to visualize how an image (analogous to an AI's total output behavior) is constructed internally by its neurons. Used to demonstrate the difference between fractured and unified representations.
EBSSC (Entropy-Bounded Sparse Semantic Calculus)	A unified formalism that integrates the geometric semantics of SpherePOP with the probabilistic control of Latent Policy Selection. It treats thought and inference as policy-driven evolutions on semantic spheres, constrained by entropy and sparsity budgets.
Epistemic Thought	Cognition aimed at reducing uncertainty about a generative world model, rather than achieving a pragmatic goal. It is formalized as the "epistemic value" term in the expected free-energy equation, which acts as an information bonus for exploratory policies.
Extractive Platform	A social platform formalized as a field theory where visibility potential (Φ) is artificially scarce, user agency (v) is opposed by platform gradients, and user effort increases informational entropy (S), which benefits the platform.
Fractured &amp; Entangled Representations	Internal representations of an AI system, typically resulting from conventional SGD training, that do not capture the underlying modularity, symmetries, and regularities of the world, even if the system's output behavior is correct.
Free-Energy Principle	A theory framing biological agents as systems that act to minimize a variational bound on surprise (free energy). In the policy selection context, agents select policies that minimize expected free energy, balancing pragmatic risk with epistemic value.
Functorial Alignment	An advanced form of invariant alignment where the alignment map is a functor F : A → V from the AI's cognitive stack (A) to the human value stack (V). Alignment is preserved if this functor remains constant up to homotopy under optimization.
Invariant Alignment	An AI alignment theory proposing that safety requires the preservation of a system's "minimal cognitive invariant" across its entire optimization trajectory. Misaligned goals must be topologically unreachable states, not merely unvisited ones.
Invariant Alignment Fixed-Point Theorem	A central theorem stating that a cognitive system remains aligned under all optimization pressure if and only if its optimization trajectories remain within the stabilizer group of its core cognitive invariant.
LASSO (Least Absolute Shrinkage and Selection Operator)	An optimization objective that includes an L1 penalty term. In the policy selection framework, this is used to model a metabolic or complexity cost, which enforces sparsity in the selection of policy atoms.
Media-Quine Closure	A principle in the PlenumHub model ensuring cross-modal completeness of a semantic sphere. A sphere is "closed" if all its required modalities (e.g., text, audio, proof) are populated, a process which can be enforced by applying modality transducers.
Open-Ended Search	An evolutionary or curriculum-driven process, exemplified by Picbreeder, that does not optimize for a single, fixed objective. It is proposed as a method for discovering "unified and factored" representations that are modular, adaptable, and robust.
Plenum	A foundational concept in EBSSC and RSVP, representing the underlying space or medium for semantic and cognitive dynamics. It is often modeled as a smooth manifold containing interacting fields for semantic potential (Φ), agency flow (v), and entropy (S).
PlenumHub	A proposed "coherence-first" computational substrate for modular knowledge systems, designed to combat semantic drift. It uses semantic spheres, the SpherePOP calculus, Media-Quine closure, and a crypto-economic layer of Texture and Time Crystals.
Policy Binding	A mechanism in the nonlinear RSVP framework where the co-activation of spatially overlapping policy atoms generates emergent interference patterns. This process is the proposed field-theoretic origin of abstraction, analogy, and creative recombination.
Policy Selection	The foundational theory that all cognitive processes—from reasoning to planning—can be formalized as sparse Bayesian selection of policies within a latent action space, optimized through free-energy minimization.
RSVP (Relativistic Scalar–Vector Plenum)	A dynamical substrate used to provide a concrete physical and mathematical instantiation for theories of policy selection and platform extraction. It models cognition and social dynamics via coupled field equations for a scalar potential (Φ), a vector flow (v), and an entropy field (S).
Scalar Potential (Φ)	A scalar field in the RSVP framework representing semantic potential, pragmatic affordance, visibility capacity, or information content.
Semantic Sphere	A core data structure in EBSSC and PlenumHub representing a bounded, structured unit of meaning. It is formally a tuple containing an internal semantic field, a boundary, required modalities, an entropy signature, and a provenance graph.
Sparsity	A core constraint in EBSSC and Policy Selection, enforcing computational and cognitive economy. It emerges from L1 penalties in optimization objectives, leading to the activation of a minimal number of policy components necessary to achieve a goal.
Sparsity Phase Transition	A sharp critical threshold in policy selection where, as the sparsity penalty (Λ) crosses a critical value (λc), the number of active policies collapses discontinuously. This marks a phase change between expressive, agentic cognition and passive prediction or silence.
SpherePOP Operators	A set of primitive operators in the EBSSC and PlenumHub calculi that define transformations on semantic spheres. Key operators include pop (expansion/transduction), merge (fusion), collapse (pruning), and bind (causal linking).
Texture Crystals (TC)	A currency in the PlenumHub system that measures the cross-modal semantic coherence of a sphere. It rewards non-redundant, well-organized structure by penalizing high mutual information between different modality payloads.
Time Crystals (TiC)	A currency in the PlenumHub system that measures the influence-weighted persistence of a semantic contribution over time. Its value decays exponentially unless reinforced by new, dependent contributions.
Unified &amp; Factored Representations	Ideal internal representations for an AI that are modular, disentangled, and capture the underlying symmetries and regularities of the world. They are associated with greater adaptability, creativity, and generalization.
