<h2 data-start="72" data-end="83">Abstract</h2>
<p data-start="85" data-end="779">This project presents a <strong data-start="109" data-end="212">physically grounded framework for modeling cognition, AI behavior, and collective knowledge systems</strong>. It formulates thought as <strong data-start="239" data-end="300">sparse policy selection constrained by entropy and energy</strong>, operating within a <strong data-start="321" data-end="339">semantic field</strong> defined by potential, agency, and uncertainty. The framework prioritizes <strong data-start="413" data-end="460">coherence, modularity, and interpretability</strong> over pattern-matching or engagement-driven optimization. Key contributions include a formal model of cognition, an entropy-bounded calculus for structured reasoning, an alignment principle based on <strong data-start="659" data-end="691">unrepresentable misalignment</strong>, and design foundations for <strong data-start="720" data-end="778">non-extractive, coherence-preserving knowledge systems</strong>.</p>
